 reads check runs (checks: read), commit statuses (commit statuses: read) and the branch rules (metadata). The merger needs read to see required checks; the owner kept write so ard can later publish its own check runs, but nothing in Phase 1 writes checks. Leaving out the `workflows` permission makes GitHub itself reject any push that edits `.github/workflows/`, a second backstop behind a guard that already refuses all of `.github/`, and it also keeps the bot from editing the bot-commit audit. The scratch-repo run (#85) confirmed that GitHub rejects such a push (\"refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow ... without `workflows` permission\"), and that these work with exactly this permission set: label writes, PR create, review and mark-ready, pinning the status issue, and timeline and collaborator-permission reads. A personal token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`) is accepted only for local `--dry-run` development, and the client is then made read-only so no write can reach GitHub.\nconfiguration.md\ngetting-started.md\nindex.md\ninstall.md\noperations\noperations.md\nrequirements.txt\nroles.md\nsecurity.md\ntroubleshooting.md\ndocs/proposal.md\ndocs/layout.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RgVixbNhHngZyneG7MVRNF","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
